{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And HaShem said to Abram: Go for yourself</b> — for your pleasure and for your benefit, and there I will make you a great nation, and here you are not meriting children. This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 12:1). But there is no need for this, for the rule of the language is indeed so: \"the rain is over and gone\" (Song of Songs 2:11), \"let me go for myself to the great ones\" (Jeremiah 5:5), \"arise and cross for yourselves the Valley of Zered\" (Deuteronomy 2:13), and most of them are like this. But our Rabbis (Yoma 3b) made a midrash from what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 10:1), \"and you shall make for yourself an ark of wood,\" and \"make for yourself two silver trumpets\" (Numbers 10:2) — because the labor is not his own, and it would have been fitting to say as he said concerning the Tabernacle, \"and the Tabernacle you shall make\" (Exodus 26:1)." "<b>From your land and from your birthplace</b> — Rashi wrote (Rashi on Genesis 12:2): but had he not already departed from there with his father and come as far as Haran? Rather, thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: distance yourself further from your father's house. And Rabbi Abraham explained that HaShem had already said to Abram, \"Go for yourself from your land,\" for this utterance was while he was still in Ur of the Chaldees, and there He commanded him to leave his land and his birthplace and his father's house which was there. But this is not correct, for if this were so, Abram would have been the primary mover of the journey from his father's house by the command of Elohim, and Terah his father went with him of his own will, whereas Scripture says (Genesis 11:31), \"And Terah took Abram his son,\" indicating that Abram went after his father and at his counsel when he departed from Ur of the Chaldees to go to the land of Canaan. Furthermore, the verse that says (Joshua 24:3), \"And I took your father Abraham from beyond the river and led him through all the land of Canaan\" — it would have been fitting to say, \"And I took your father Abraham from Ur of the Chaldees and led him through all the land of Canaan,\" for from there he was taken and there he was commanded concerning this. And further, it will be difficult for them, for Abraham in commanding Eliezer to take a wife for his son said to him, \"for to my land and to my birthplace you shall go\" (Genesis 24:4), and he went \"to Aram Naharaim, to the city of Nahor\" (ibid. 24:10) — if so, that is his land and his birthplace, and there it is stated (Genesis 24:38), \"but you shall go to my father's house and to my family,\" for there is his father's house and his family, which is his birthplace — not as Rabbi Abraham erred in saying \"to my land\" is Haran and \"my birthplace\" is Ur of the Chaldees. And behold, he himself says here that in Ur of the Chaldees it was said to him, \"Go for yourself from your land and from your birthplace and from your father's house\" — and behold, he would have many lands! But the correct view you already know from what we wrote in the preceding parashah (11:28), that Haran is his land and there is his birthplace, and it is the land of his fathers from of old, and there he was commanded to leave them. And thus they said in Bereshit Rabbah (Bereshit Rabbah 39:8): \"Go for yourself\" — one from Aram Naharaim and one from Aram Nahor. And the reason for mentioning \"your land and your birthplace and your father's house\" is that it is hard for a person to leave his land in which he dwells and where his loved ones and friends are, and all the more so when it is the land of his birthplace where he was born, and all the more so when his entire father's house is there. Therefore it was necessary to say to him that he should leave everything for the love of the Holy One, blessed be He." "<b>To the land that I will show you</b> — he would wander and journey from nation to nation and from kingdom to another people, until he came to the land of Canaan and He said to him, \"To your seed I will give this land\" — then \"to the land that I will show you\" was fulfilled, and then he stopped and settled in it. And what it says, \"and they went to go to the land of Canaan,\" was not for permanent settlement, for he did not yet know that it was upon that land he had been commanded; rather, the righteous one grasped his path toward the land of Canaan, for so it was in his mind and in his father's mind also from the beginning when they departed from Ur of the Chaldees. And because of this he said, \"when Elohim caused me to wander from my father's house\" (Genesis 20:13), for he was wandering like one lost. And it is possible to say that Abraham from the start knew that the land of Canaan is the inheritance of HaShem and there HaShem would give him his portion, and he believed that \"to the land that I will show you\" alludes to the land of Canaan, whether to all of it or to one of all the lands of El, and he set his face toward the general direction of the land of Canaan, for there is the land that He will truly show him."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The page reference יומה ג עב is to the Talmudic folio format; rendered as 3b.", 1 "In the phrase 'and there it is stated (Genesis 24:38),' Ramban cites the verse number as ל״ח (38), which indeed matches Genesis 24:38, not Genesis 12:38 as the verse numbering in the source's parenthetical might suggest; the citation is correct to chapter 24."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:28:25.411084385Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}